## What is Ruby Raider?

Ruby Raider is a generator and scaffolding gem to make UI test automation easier.

### Supported Frameworks

| Web Testing Framework | Mobile Testing Framework           |
|-----------------------|------------------------------------|
| Cucumber and Selenium | Cucumber and Appium for iOS        |
| RSpec and Selenium    | RSpec and Appium for iOS           |
| Cucumber and Watir    | Cucumber and Appium for Android    |
| RSpec and Watir       | RSpec and Appium for Android       |
|                       | Cucumber and Appium Cross-platform |
|                       | RSpec and Appium Cross-platform    |

**Optional add-on for web projects:**
- **Accessibility testing (axe)**: `--accessibility` flag

***The web tests run against the [Raider Test Store](https://raider-test-site.onrender.com/).***

***To run Appium tests, download the example [app](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_test_app) and start the server:***

```bash
raider u start_appium
```

This works on all platforms (Mac OS, Linux and Windows).

## Getting Started

```bash
gem install ruby_raider
raider new [name_of_project]
```

Or with parameters:

```bash
raider new [name_of_project] -p framework:[framework] automation:[automation_type]
```

### Commands

```
raider generate         # Scaffolding commands
raider utility          # Utility commands
raider new [NAME]       # Create new project
raider version          # Show version
raider help [COMMAND]   # Describe commands
```

Shortcuts: `g` (generate), `n` (new), `u` (utility), `v` (version)

### Scaffolding

```
raider g page [NAME]      # Create page object
raider g spec [NAME]      # Create RSpec test
raider g feature [NAME]   # Create Cucumber feature
raider g steps [NAME]     # Create step definitions
raider g scaffold [NAME]  # Create page + test + steps
```

## Reporting

<%- if rspec? -%>
Every run writes machine-readable results to `raider-results/` (including `run.json`) and a browsable report,
`raider-results/index.html`. Open it in a browser after a run; `bundle exec raider-reporter report` rebuilds it.
<%- else -%>
Every run writes machine-readable results to `raider-results/` (including `run.json`) and a browsable report,
`raider-results/index.html`. The reporter runs in plugin mode:
`features/support/env.rb` requires `raider_reporter/cucumber`, so no extra Cucumber formatter flag is needed.
<%- end -%>

To upload runs to Raider, set these environment variables before running the suite:

```bash
export RAIDER_TOKEN=your-token
export RAIDER_ENDPOINT=https://your-raider-endpoint
```

<%- if rspec? -%>
Without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, nothing is uploaded and the suite output is unchanged. Failure screenshots are attached to the
report and to Raider. See the [raider_reporter README](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_reporter) for details.
<%- else -%>
Without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, nothing is uploaded and the suite output is unchanged. Failure screenshots are attached to the
report and to Raider. See the [raider_reporter README](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_reporter) for details.
<%- end -%>

<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quarantine

CI runs the suite through `bundle exec raider-reporter exec -- …` with `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` set. Tests quarantined in
Raider still run and are still reported (as `quarantined`), and a build whose only failures are quarantined stays green.
Local runs keep quarantine off. To turn it off in CI, remove `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` from the pipeline.

<%- end -%>
<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quality gate

The `<%= azure? ? 'raider_gate' : 'raider-gate' %>` CI job runs `bundle exec raider-reporter gate` after the tests and
fails the pipeline when the Raider quality gate fails. It is off until you turn it on:

- set the CI variable `RAIDER_GATE=1` (<%= azure? ? 'a pipeline variable' : 'a repository variable under Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables' %>);
- turn on rules under the project's Gate page in Raider: a minimum pass rate, no new failures against the default
  branch, no new flaky tests;
- optionally require the `raider/gate` check in branch protection.

`raider-reporter gate` exits 1 when the gate fails, naming the failed rules, and 2 when it gets no verdict (timeout,
Raider unreachable, token rejected, build not found). `RAIDER_GATE_TIMEOUT` sets how long it waits, in seconds
(default 600). The job stays off by default because a project without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, or a pull request from a fork,
would exit 2 on every run.

<%- end -%>
<%- if azure? && mobile? -%>
## CI

Azure Pipelines template for mobile projects is not available yet. Use GitHub Actions (`ci:github`) or write your own pipeline; see the raider_reporter README for the Azure setup.

<%- elsif azure? -%>
## Azure Pipelines

`azure-pipelines.yml` runs the suite on `ubuntu-latest` and, on pull requests, comments the Raider results.

- Create a pipeline from `azure-pipelines.yml`, then add `RAIDER_TOKEN` (mark it secret) and `RAIDER_ENDPOINT` as pipeline variables.
- Contribute to pull requests: grant the pipeline's build service identity (`<Project> Build Service (<Organization>)`) the "Contribute to pull requests" permission on the repository, so `raider-reporter comment` can post the comment and status.
- Azure Repos ignores `pr:` triggers. Add this pipeline as a Build validation branch policy so pull request builds (and the Raider comment) run.
- Keep the `raider_comment` job in the same stage as the `tests` job.
- Pull request comments work for Azure Repos only; a GitHub-hosted repo built by Azure Pipelines gets results uploaded but no PR comment.
- Self-hosted agent? Replace `vmImage: ubuntu-latest` with `pool: { name: Default }`.

<%- end -%>
